## ABSTRACT
Rational construction of hierarchical multi-component materials with abundant heterostructure is evolving as a promising strategy to achieve excellent metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) based electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorbers. Herein, hierarchical heterostructure \(\mathsf{W S}_{2}/\mathsf{C o S}_{2}\) @carbonized cotton fiber (CCF) was fabricated using the ZIF-67 MOFs nanosheets anchored cotton fiber (ZIF-67@CF) as a precursor through the tungsten etching, sulfurization, and carbonization process. Apart from the synergetic effect of dielectric-magnetic dual-loss mechanism, the hierarchical heterostructure and multicomponent of \(\mathsf{W S}_{2}/\mathsf{C o S}_{2}\ @\mathsf{C C F}\) also display improved impedance matching. Furthermore, numerous W-S-Co bands and heterojunction interfaces of heterogeneous \(\mathsf{W S}_{2}/\mathsf{C o S}_{2}\) are beneficial to promoting additional interfacial/dipole polarization loss and conductive loss, thereby enhancing the EMW attenuation performance. Based on the percolation theory, a good balance between impedance matching and EMW absorption capacity was achieved for the \(\mathsf{W S}_{2}/\mathsf{C o S}_{2}\ @\mathsf{C C F}/\) paraffin composite with 20 wt. \(\%\) filler loading, exhibiting strong EMW absorption capability with a minimum reflection loss \((\mathsf{R L}_{\mathsf{m i n}})\) value of \(-51.26\) dB at 17.36 GHz with \(2\ \mathsf{m m}\) thickness and a maximum effective absorption bandwidth \(\left(\mathsf{E A B}_{\mathsf{m a x}}\right)\) as wide as 6.72 GHz. Our research will provide new guidance for designing high-efficient MOFs derived EMW absorbers.
## KEYWORDS
hierarchical, heterostructure, sulfurization, metal-organic framework, microwave absorber
## 1 Introduction
With the advent of fifth generation (5G) information age, electromagnetic interference (EMI) and radiation produced by electronic communication devices have become a serious issue in human health, electronic information safety, and device performance [1–6]. Thus, it is urgent to explore low-cost and high-efficient electromagnetic microwave wave (EMW) absorbers, which could effectively attenuate the incident EMW by transforming them into heat or other forms of energy. Normally, ideal EMW absorbing materials should possess the essential characteristics of thin matching thickness, broad bandwidth, strong absorption, and light weight [5–9]. To meet the above requirements, reasonable structure and component design have drawn considerable attention in advanced EMW absorbers.
Nowadays, carbon materials including carbon nanotubes [10], graphene [11], and carbon aerogels [12] have been widely used in the field of EMW absorption due to their high dielectric loss, low
density, and good environmental stability. Nevertheless, the complex synthesis process and high cost of them have seriously hindered their large-scale applications. Owing to the merits of sustainability, low-cost, nontoxicity, and facile process, the pyrolysis products of biomass materials such as platanus [13], wood [14], cattail [15], and lotus leaf [16] have been widely considered as the new carbon based EMW absorbing materials. More importantly, their special intrinsic structures (e.g., cellular structure, hierarchical structure, interwoven network structure, and hollow tube-like structure) and amounts of defects and residual functional groups are also helpful for extending transmission path through multiple reflections and generating enhanced polarization loss, exhibiting superior EMW absorption performance. However, the high conductivity of carbon materials can also usually bring serious impedance mismatching that leads to unexpected reflection of incident EMW.
Generally, optimized impedance matching could be effectively
achieved through engineering magnetic/dielectric multicomponent materials with rational structure design. In addition, the dielectric and magnetic dual-loss mechanism is also beneficial for improved loss capacity simultaneously. With the rapid development of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) materials, carbon/metal composites prepared from calcinated MOFs present great application potential in EMW absorption because of their merits of high porosity, controllable structure, and adjustable composition. Meanwhile, MOFs modified fiber and fabric have also been widely investigated and used as a precursor for carbonization, which could effectively avoid the discontinuity and aggregation of single calcinated MOFs particles, constructing high-efficient electromagnetic attenuation network easily. What's more, amounts of heterogeneous interfaces can also be simultaneously constructed to contribute additional interfacial polarization loss, boosting the EMW absorption capability. For example, Wang et al. used cotton fiber (CF) and Co-ZIF as precursors to fabricate the heterostructure Co/CNTs-MXene@CF through the self-assembly and thermocatalytic technology, achieving a minimum reflection loss \(\mathrm{(RL_{min})}\) of \(-61.41\) dB at \(2.52\ \mathrm{mm}\) and a broad maximum effective absorption bandwidth \(\mathrm{(EAB_{max})}\) of \(5.04\ \mathrm{GHz}\) [17]. Xu et al. synthesized CC/ZIFs with abundant heterogeneous interfaces and defects derived from CoZn-ZIF and cotton fiber cloth, showing a \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) of \(-36.0\) dB and \(\mathrm{EAB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) up to \(11.6\,\mathrm{GHz}\) [18].
In this study, hierarchical heterostructure \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{EAB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) of \(5.04\ \mathrm{GHz}\) [17]. Xu et al. synthesized CC/ZIFs with abundant heterogeneous interfaces and defects derived from CoZn-ZIF and cotton fiber cloth, showing a \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) of \(-36.0\) dB and \(\mathrm{EAB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) up to \(11.6\,\mathrm{GHz}\) [18].
## 2 Experimental sections
## 2.1 Materials and chemicals
Pure cotton face towel (Huizhou Jianrou Industrial Co., Ltd.) was bought from supermarket and served as the biomass carbon resource. 2-Methylimidazole was purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{WO}_{4}\) were supplied by Tianjin Kemizu Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Thiourea was provided by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All the materials and chemicals were used as received without further purification.
## 2.2 Preparation procedure
Synthesis of ZIF-67@CF: According to our previous work, ZIF-67 precursor solution was first prepared by dissolving \(0.58\,\mathrm{~g~}\) of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(1.31\,\mathrm{~g~}\) of 2-methylimidazole into \(40\,\mathrm{~mL}\) deionized (DI) water under vigorous magnetic stirring for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) . Then, a piece of cotton face towel \((4\ \mathrm{cm}\times4\ \mathrm{cm})\) was vertically immersed into the obtained purple ZIF-67 precursor solution for \(4\ \mathrm{h}\) at \(25\ ^{\circ}C\) to grow ZIF-67 onto the CF surface, followed by washing with DI water and ethanol, and drying at \(40\,^{\circ}\!C\) overnight, obtaining the purple ZIF-67@CF.
Preparation of \(W S_{2}/C o S_{2}@C C F\) : \(0.2\mathrm{~g~Na}_{2}\mathrm{WO}_{4}\) was dissolved thoroughly in \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) DI water and uniformly mixed with \(80~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol, and the obtained ZIF-67@CF was then immersed into the mixed solution at \(85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until the purple color disappeared completely. After being rinsed with DI water and ethanol, and dried at \(40\,^{\circ}\!C,\) it was placed in the center of tube furnace with \(1\;\mathrm{g}\) of sulfur powder at the upstream and calcinated at \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere \((30\mathrm{\mL/min})\) for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) with a heating rate of \(5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min},\) obtaining the designed \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) heterostructure material. For comparation, CCF and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) were also prepared by calcinating the CF and ZIF-67@CF directly under the same condition, respectively.
## 2.3 Characterizations
After etching with \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{WO}_{4}\) solution, the basic morphology structure of ZIF-67 is well maintained (Fig. 2(b)), but the smooth surface turns to be wrinkled with lots of obvious ridges (Figs. 2(e) and 2(h)), which can be ascribed to the collapse of MOF framework. For \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\), smaller and denser arranged nanosheets are clearly observed (Fig. 2(c)) because of the shrinkage of CF and MOF framework after the high temperature pyrolysis treatment. From the magnification images in Figs. 2(f) and 2(i), it can be observed that the triangular nanosheet turns to be rougher and looser, whose subunits are assembled by tightly stacked nanoflakes with numerous nano-sized pores. Compared with the surface of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) anchored with amounts of nanoparticles (Fig. S3 in the ESM), the unique hierarchical porous structure is beneficial for optimizing the impedance matching and accelerating the multiple reflection of incident EMW, achieving the improved EMW absorption capacity. In addition, the cross connected carbonized triangular nanosheets and the tightly stacked conductive \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoflakes can also accelerate electron transfer to increase the conduction loss effect.
More detail structure information of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) was further investigated. As shown in Fig. 3(a), it can be clearly seen from the TEM image that the triangular nanosheet contains numerous nanoflakes, which is in good agreement with the SEM images. From the high resolution TEM (HRTEM) image of the nanoflake in Fig. 3(b), we can see a visible parallel lattice fringe with an interplanar distance of \(0.62\ \mathrm{nm}\), which can be indexed to
the (002) crystal plane of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}\), while the lattice spacings of 0.32 and \(0.25\ \mathrm{nm}\) correspond to the (111) and (210) crystal planes of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) [19]. Notably, the long-range continuous interfaces between \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}\) can be clearly observed, and the intermediate S atoms act as bridge to connect W and Co, providing multiple electron transfer paths. Energy dispersive X-ray spectrometer (EDS) pattern of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) was also conducted (Fig. S4 in the ESM). As illustrated in Fig. 3(c), the elements of C, N, Co, W, and S are homogeneously distributed on the whole fiber surface,
further demonstrating the successful fabrication of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\). Therefore, the hierarchical porous structure, abundant heterojunction interfaces, conductive carbonaceous component, and heteroatom doping of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) will undoubtedly endow it with great potential for EMW absorption application.
To analyze the phase information and crystal structure of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\), XRD patterns of them were performed and illustrated in Fig. 4(a). The broad diffraction peaks at about \(25^{\circ}\) for the two samples can be assigned to the (002) plane of graphite carbon derived from the pyrolyzed CF and MOF skeleton. For \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\), the obvious representative diffraction peaks at \(32.3^{\circ}\), \(36.0^{\circ}\), \(46.3^{\circ}\), and \(54.7^{\circ}\) correspond to the (200), (210), (220), and (311) crystal planes of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) (JCPDS card: 41-1471) [19], respectively. From the pattern of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\), apart from the typical diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\), the others at \(14.4^{\circ}\), \(43.7^{\circ}\), and \(58.0^{\circ}\) are indexed to the (002), (104), and (110) planes of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}\) (JCPDS card: 08-0337) [20], respectively. All these results suggest that \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) have been successfully prepared and incorporated into the designed high temperature sulfurization product of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\).
Surface chemical composition and valence states of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) were further analyzed via XPS measurement. From the XPS full-survey-scan spectrum shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, it can be confirmed that the main elements of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) are Co, O, N, C, S, and W, which are coincided with the EDS results. Specifically, the high solution C 1s spectrum can be split into three peaks at 284.8, 285.7, and \(288.2~\mathrm{eV}\), which are corresponded to the \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{C},\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{C},\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{N},\) and \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) groups (Fig. 4(b)) [21], respectively. In general, all these carbon bonds are beneficial for fast electron transfer to achieve good conductivity. For the Co 2p spectrum (Fig. 4(c)), the peaks at 778.6 and \(793.8~\mathrm{eV}\) are assigned to the \(\mathrm{Co}^{+}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{+}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) of \(\mathrm{Co}^{+}\), respectively. The peaks at 780.3 and \(797.2~\mathrm{eV}\) are ascribed to the presence of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) valence state, while there are also two satellite (sat.) peaks at 784.8 and \(802.5~\mathrm{eV}\) [10, 22]. Figure 4(d) displays the deconvoluted W 4f spectrum, where the first pair of peaks at about 32.2 and \(34.3~\mathrm{eV}\) belong to W \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) and W \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) of \(\mathrm{W}^{4+}\), and two peaks (35.4 and \(37.4~\mathrm{eV}\)) in the other pair to \(\mathrm{W}^{6+}\) of \(\mathrm{WO}_{3}\) are also observed because of the oxidation [20, 23]. As presented in Fig. 4(e), the S 2p spectrum clearly exhibits the two major peaks of S \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and S \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) at about 161.9 and \(163.3~\mathrm{eV}\). And the peak at \(164.9~\mathrm{eV}\) stands for the typical metal–sulfur (M–S) bond in the material [22]. The N 1s spectrum in Fig. 4(f) displays the peaks
398.5, 400.1, and \(402.0~\mathrm{eV}\), which are related to pyridinic-N, pyrrolic-N, and graphitic-N respectively, indicating the successful N-doping in carbon matrix [24]. Here, the electronegative N atoms act as polarization center under alternating electromagnetic field to contribute dipolar relaxation loss, and the N atom can also improve the electron transport capacity through providing electron to pair with the conjugate ring, generating the conduction loss [25].
According to transmission line theory, EMW absorption capacity is usually evaluated by RL, which can be calculated using the following Eqs. (1) and (2) [12]
\[
\mathrm{RL}(\mathrm{dB})=20\log|(Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_0)/(Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_0)|
\]
\[
Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}}{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\mathrm{tanh}\left[\mathrm{j}(2\pi f\mathrm{d}/c)\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\right]}
\]
where \(Z_{\mathrm{in}}\) is the normalized input impedance of the absorber, \(Z_{0}\) refers to the impedance of free space, \(f_{\mathrm{i}}\) is the frequency of EMW, \(d\) and \(c\) represent the absorber thickness and the velocity of the microwave in free space, and \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\) and \(\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\) are the complex permittivity and permeability. General speaking, the frequency range in which the RL value is smaller than \(-10~\mathrm{dB}\) (90% attenuation of incident EM energy) is called the EAB. The RL curves, and the corresponding three-dimensional (3D) and two-dimensional (2D) contour plots of CCF, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\), and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) with a filler loading of \(20~\mathrm{wt.\%}\) in the range of 2–18 GHz are displayed in Fig. 5. As shown in Figs. 5(a), 5(d), and 5(g), CCF displays a \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of only \(-0.83~\mathrm{dB}\) at \(11.92~\mathrm{GHz}\) with a thickness of \(4.00~\mathrm{mm}\) and no available EAB, which cannot satisfy the basic engineering requirement of \(-10~\mathrm{dB}\). As for the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) (Figs. 5(b), 5(e), and 5(h)), the \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value increases to \(-36.00~\mathrm{dB}\) at \(9.84~\mathrm{GHz}\) with a thickness of \(2.95~\mathrm{mm}\) and the \(\mathrm{EAB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) covers \(5.12~\mathrm{GHz}\) from \(12.88\) to \(18~\mathrm{GHz}\) at \(1.9~\mathrm{mm}\). As expected, the \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) exhibits the strongest EMW absorption capacity with an \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of \(-51.26~\mathrm{dB}\) at \(17.36~\mathrm{GHz}\) and \(2~\mathrm{mm}\) thickness (Figs. 5(c), 5(f), and 5(i)), demonstrating that about \(99.999\%\) EMW can be consumed efficiently. Meanwhile, a broader \(\mathrm{EAB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) of \(6.72~\mathrm{GHz}\) (11.28–18 GHz) can also be achieved for the sample with \(2.35~\mathrm{mm}\) thickness, covering all the Ku band and part of X band. Hence, it can be inferred that the hierarchical porous structure and amounts of heterojunction interfaces can significantly affect the EMW absorbing performance of the absorbers.
As is well-known, EMW absorption performances of samples are closely related to the corresponding electromagnetic parameters of complex permittivity \((\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\,=\,\varepsilon^{\prime}\,-\,\mathrm{j}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime})\) and complex permeability \((\mu_{\mathrm{r}}=\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})\) . The real part \((\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\mu^{\prime}\) ) represents the electrical and magnetic energy storage capability, while the imaginary part \((\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) and \(\mu^{\prime\prime}\) ) stands for the electrical and magnetic energy dissipation ability. Besides, dielectric loss tangent \(\left(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{e}}=\right.\) \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime})\) and magnetic loss tangent \((\tan\delta_{\mathrm{u}}=\mu^{\prime\prime}/\mu^{\prime})\) were also calculated to evaluate the dielectric and magnetic dissipation ability [11]. Figure 6 displays that the electromagnetic parameters of CCF, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF},\) and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) with a \(20~\mathrm{wt.}\%\) filler loading in the frequency range of \(2{-}18~\mathrm{GHz}\) tested by the coaxial method. Obviously, as presented in Figs. 6(a) and 6(b), both the \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) values of them show a continuous downward trend with increasing frequency, revealing a typical frequency dispersion phenomenon due to the delayed charge transfer and polarization response associated with the changing electrical field [26–28]. In addition, both the \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) values of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) are much higher than those of CCF, but the \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) values of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) are remarkably different from that of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF},\) demonstrating that the introduction of hierarchical heterostructure could further tune the permittivity to achieve better impedance matching. What's more, obvious relaxation peaks are clearly observed for the \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) curves of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF},\) indicating the existence of polarization-relaxation process. In general, these peaks at low and high frequency range are mainly caused by dipole polarization and interface polarization, which are beneficial for the improved dielectric loss. Furthermore, as shown in Fig. 6(c), the \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) exhibits the highest value in the whole testing frequency range, demonstrating the strongest dielectric loss capacity.
In general, conduction loss and polarization loss contribute to the dielectric loss. Compared with CCF and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF},\) the hierarchical heterostructure of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) mainly supplies connected 3D porous conductive network and heterojunction interfaces to increase the conduction loss and interfacial polarization loss. Here, Detry theory was used to analyze the
polarization loss mechanism according to the following Eq. (3) [11]
\[
\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
\]
where the \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}\) and \(\varepsilon_{\infty}\) stand for the static permittivity and relative dielectric permittivity under high frequency limit, respectively. In general, a semicircle (Cole–Cole semicircle) in the obtained \(\varepsilon^{\prime}{-}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) curve represents a dielectric polarization relaxation process [29]. However, it always delineates nonstandard semicircle and linear tail due to the conductivity of absorber, and a longer tail indicates stronger conduction loss. As presented in Fig. 6(d) and Fig. S6 in the ESM, obvious multiple nonstandard Cole–Cole semicircles are observed for all samples, indicating the existence of polarization relaxation processes. Specifically, the residual oxygen containing functional groups and defects in CCF acts as the dipole active sites to generate the dipole polarization loss, but additional interfacial polarization loss arising from the accumulated electrons at the \(\mathrm{CCF-CoS}_{2}\) interfaces under the applied electrical field and dipole polarization loss from the doped N elements is produced for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) . As for \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF},\) high temperature solfication introduced S element and heterojunction interfaces of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) contribute stronger dipole/interfacial polarization loss. Furthermore, the linear tail which represents the existence of conduction loss is clearly observed for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF},\) but the \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) displays a much longer tail, which can be ascribed to the low-electronegativity sulfur in heterogeneous \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) that can effectively accelerate the transfer of electrons, supplying more effective conduction loss.
Moreover, as shown in Figs. S7(a) and S7(b) in the ESM, the values of \(\mu^{\prime}\) and \(\mu^{\prime\prime}\) fluctuate around 1 and 0, respectively, indicating a weak magnetic behavior. Besides, it can be observed that the \(\mu^{\prime\prime}\) and \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{u}}\) of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) exhibit a similar trend with several resonance peaks in the curves, illustrating the presence of magnetic loss. As displayed in Fig. S8 in the ESM, the eddy current coefficient \(C_{0}\)
\((C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}=2\pi\mu_{0}\alpha^{2}\sigma/3)\) [30] of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) fluctuates within the testing frequency range and tends to be horizontal in the range of \(9{-}13\ \mathrm{GHz}\), confirming that magnetic loss is caused by eddy current, natural resonance, and exchange resonance simultaneously [31, 32]. Importantly, the \(\tan\delta_{\mu}\) value in Fig. S7(c) in the ESM is much lower than the \(\tan\delta_{\varepsilon}\) value, indicating the dielectric loss dominant EMW attenuation mechanism.
General speaking, impedance matching ratio \((Z)\) and attenuation constant \((\alpha)\) are the two key factors to affect the EMW absorption capacity of materials. Impedance matching ratio is closely related to the ability of incident EMW to enter the interior of absorbers, and a \(Z\) value close to 1 means that almost all the EMW can penetrate the absorbers without being reflected, showing an ideal impedance matching. The \(Z\) values of CCF, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\), and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) were calculated according to the following Eq. (4) [10, 33]
\[
z=\sqrt{\sqrt{(\mu^{\prime\prime2}+\mu^{\prime2})}/\sqrt{(\varepsilon^{\prime2}+\varepsilon^{\prime2})}}
\]
As illustrated in Fig. 6(e), the \(Z\) value of CCF is closest to 1 among all samples, showing the best impedance matching. But the \(Z\) values of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) are much lower than CCF, demonstrating the worse impedance matching due to their increased conductivity according to the \(\varepsilon^{\prime}{-}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) curves. However, \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) with higher conductivity displays a better impedance matching than that of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\), which can be attributed to the special hierarchical porous structure that is beneficial for the improved impedance matching. Furthermore, \(\alpha\) was also evaluated to analyze the attention capacity through the following Eq. (5) [34, 35]
\[
\alpha=\frac{\pi f\sqrt{2}}{c}\times\sqrt{(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime})}+\sqrt{(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime})^{2}+(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}+\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime})^{2}}
\]
Obviously, as shown in Fig. 6(f), the \(\alpha\) values of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) are much higher than CCF, and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) displays the highest value. Hence, \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) possesses the strongest EMW attenuation capacity due to its special hierarchical porous structure and amounts of heterojunctions. As a result, appropriate impedance matching and strong EMW attenuation capacity endow
\(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) with excellent EMW absorption performance compared with other two samples.
Furthermore, series of paraffin composites with different \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading were also prepared to investigate their EMW absorption performance. As displayed in Fig. S9 in the ESM, the paraffin composites with \(10\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) are provided with an \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of \(-17.52\ \mathrm{dB}\), which is slightly decreased to \(-19.86\ \mathrm{dB}\) for a \(15\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) loading. Then, it exhibits a significant decrease to \(-51.26\ \mathrm{dB}\) for \(20\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) loading, showing a great improvement in EMW absorption (Fig. 5(f)). But an obvious degraded absorption is observed with further increasing the \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading, and an \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of \(-17.83\) and \(-15.46\ \mathrm{dB}\) is obtained for \(25\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) and \(30\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) loading. Besides, the statical \(\mathrm{EAB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) values are \(3.04\), \(5.92\), \(5.72\), \(6.4\), and \(6.24\ \mathrm{GHz}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), \(15\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), \(20\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), \(25\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), and \(30\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) loading, respectively. For the dielectric loss dominant absorber, the permittivities of paraffin composites with different \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading were further analyzed to explain the typical filler loading dependent EMW absorption performance. As displayed in Fig. 7(a) and Fig. S10(a) in the ESM, both \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) increase with increasing \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading in the frequency range of \(2{-}18\ \mathrm{GHz}\), and the \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) values at \(2\ \mathrm{GHz}\) as a function of the \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading display a typical percolation behavior (Fig. 7(b) and Fig. S10(b) in the ESM) [29]. Specifically, the value of \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) shows a subtle increase from \(5.5\) to \(6.7\) and \(2.2\) to \(3.0\) for the lower \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading range of \(10\ \mathrm{wt.\%}{-}15\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), and then a sharp increase in \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) from \(6.7\) to \(13.6\) and \(3.0\) to \(9.0\) is observed in the filler loading range of \(15\ \mathrm{wt.\%}{-}25\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\). After that, the growth rate of them slows down again from the \(25\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading. Hence, it can be induced that the filler loading range of \(15\ \mathrm{wt.\%}{-}25\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) is the percolation region, which is closely related with the filler loading dependent EMW absorption performance.
Based on the percolation theory, the corresponding mechanism is illustrated in Fig. 7(c). The dispersed low-content \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) is isolated by paraffin where the interface between them can serve as micro-capacitor to store electric charge (Fig. 7(c)(i)). Meanwhile, the accumulated unbalanced charge at the interfaces can also result in the interfacial polarization loss under alternating electric field, which is a main attenuation mode towards the incident EMW. With the further increase in \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) loading, more heterojunction interfaces are
produced, both the electrical energy storage and interfacial polarization loss capability are significantly enhanced. Besides, partial \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) also turns to be connected with each other to construct the local microcircuit, generating the additional conduction loss which is beneficial for improved EMW absorption performances (Fig. 7(c)(ii)). When the \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) loading exceeds a certain content (percolation threshold), stable conductive network is constructed, and the increase in conduction loss slows down. This can also be verified by the Cole–Cole curves in Fig. 7(d) that the paraffin composites with 20 wt.% \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) possess the most semicircles, but a longer linear tail is observed for the composites with higher loading, indicating the improved conductivity, which is coincided with the result shown in Fig. 7(e). However, excessive \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) loading means stable conductive network and much higher conductivity (Fig. 7(c)(iii)), which will undoubtedly lead to poor impedance matching and reduce EMW absorption capacity. As a result, a 20 wt.% filler loading is the most optimal value to achieve the strongest EMW absorption.
Remarkably, the absorption peaks shifted to low frequency with increasing sample thickness from 2 to \( 5\,\textrm{m m} \), which can be explained by the 1/4 quarter wavelength cancellation formula (Eq. (6)) [36, 37]
\[
t_\mathrm{m}=n\lambda/4=nc/\left(4f_\mathrm{m}\sqrt{|\mu_\mathrm{r}||\varepsilon_\mathrm{r}|}\right)(n=1,3,5\ldots)
\]
where \( t_{\mathrm{m}} \) and \( f_{\mathrm{m}} \) correspond to the matching thickness and matching frequency, respectively. When \( t_{\mathrm{m}} \) and \( f_{\mathrm{m}} \) conform to the
equation, the incident and reflected wave at the interface of air and absorber are out of phase by \( 180^{\circ} \), enabling the total cancellation of reflected EMW and best EMW absorption capacity. As shown in Fig. 8(a), the blue heart symbols that correspond to the matching thickness of \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) under 20 wt.% filler loading are well coincided with simulated red line, indicating the EMW absorption capability of our prepared hierarchical heterostructure \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) conforms to the quarter-wavelength cancellation model which endows it with the best EMW absorption performances. Furthermore, EMW absorption performances of our prepared hierarchical heterostructure \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) were compared with other reported carbon-based magnetic-dielectric absorbers [38–46]. As displayed in Figs. 8(b) and 8(c), \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) exhibits a strong absorption \((-51.26\,\mathrm{~dB})\) at a thickness of only \( 2\,\textrm{m m} \) and wide EAB \((6.72\,\mathrm{GHz})\) at low filler loading \((20\,\mathrm{wt.\%})\), which are superior to other absorbers. Generally, smaller matching thickness and lower filler loading imply a reduction of weight, which is just needed for the practical application. Thus, it can be claimed that the \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) has great application potential for high-efficient EMW absorption.
Based on the above analysis, the EMW absorption mechanism of \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \) is displayed in Fig. 9. Firstly, the low-electronegativity sulfur in heterogeneous \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2} \) can effectively accelerate the transfer of electrons in the hierarchical heterostructure \( \mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF} \), generating significant heterojunction-induced conduction loss through the connected 3D conductive network. Secondly, the unique hierarchical porous structure is conducive to optimize the impedance matching and
facilitate multiple scattering and reflection of incident microwave, further enhancing the attenuation capacity. Meanwhile, amounts of heterogenous interfaces of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) and contact interfaces of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) -CCF and \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) -paraffin can also induce powerful interface polarization loss. Furthermore, the residual functional groups, defects, and doped \(_\mathrm{N}\) and S elements in \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) act as active centers to promote the formation of dipoles under alternating electromagnetic filed, generating the dipole polarization loss. Finally, the presence of magnetic \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoflakes can also endow the hierarchical heterostructure material with suitable magnetic loss performance, such as exchange resonance, eddy current loss, and natural resonance, which can further enhance the dissipation of EMW energy due to the synergistic effect of magnetic and dielectric loss. As a result, the multiple loss mechanisms make the hierarchical heterostructure \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) composite with excellent EMW absorption performance, thus providing a new route for the accurate design of advanced EMW absorbers.
## 4 Conclusions
To sum up, we designed a hierarchical heterostructure \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) with outstanding EMW performance, which was successfully prepared by using biomass resources and ZIF-67 as
precursors through a facile and controllable etching, sulfurization, and carbonization approach. As a result, the prepared \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{CCF}\) shows a prominent \(\mathrm{PL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) of \(-51.26~\mathrm{dB}\) with a thickness of \(2\;\mathrm{mm}\) and a wider EAB of \(6.72\:\mathrm{GHz}\) \((11.28{-}18\,\mathrm{GHz})\) when the filler loading is \(20~\mathrm{wt.}\%\) . The coexistence of magnetic and dielectric loss, special heterostructure and multicomponent are conducive to optimizing impedance matching. Moreover, the amounts of heterogenous \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) are also advantageous to increase dipole/interfacial polarization loss and conduction loss, generating improved EMW attenuation capability. Overall, this work offers a low-cost and novel method for designing hierarchical heterostructure materials for high-performance EMW absorbers.
